## Proof

**Model.** A hydra is a simple graph $G=(V,E)$: heads are vertices, necks are edges, $|E|=100$. Hitting head $A$ toggles all edges incident to $A$ (removes existing ones, adds all missing ones), i.e. it replaces $G$ by $G\oplus \mathrm{st}(A)$, where $\mathrm{st}(A)$ is the star of $A$ (edges incident to $A$) and $\oplus$ is symmetric difference over $\mathbb F_2$.

Hitting a set $S\subseteq V$ (in any order) transforms $G$ into
$$G\oplus \delta(S),\qquad \delta(S):=\bigoplus_{v\in S}\mathrm{st}(v),$$
the cut between $S$ and $V\setminus S$ (since $\bigoplus_v \mathrm{st}(v)=0$, stars generate the cut space). Operations commute and are involutions, so only the parity of hits at each vertex matters. We seek
$$N=\max_{|E|=100}\;\min\{|S|: G\oplus\delta(S)\text{ is disconnected}\}.$$

**Lemma (disconnection criterion).** $G\oplus\delta(S)$ is disconnected iff there exists a nonempty proper subset $T\subsetneq V$ such that the cross-adjacency block $A[T,V\setminus T]$ over $\mathbb F_2$ has rank $\le 1$. In that case the working sets $S$ are exactly $\{v:c(v)=1\}$ and its complement, where $c:V\to\{0,1\}$ satisfies $A[u,v]=c(u)\oplus c(v)$ for all $u\in T,\;v\notin T$.

*Proof.* $G\oplus\delta(S)$ is disconnected iff some nontrivial $T$ has no crossing edges in $G\oplus\delta(S)$. For $u\in T,v\notin T$, edge $uv$ lies in $G\oplus\delta(S)$ iff $A[u,v]\oplus(\mathbf 1_S(u)\oplus \mathbf 1_S(v))=0$, i.e. $A[u,v]=c(u)\oplus c(v)$ with $c=\mathbf 1_S$. This holding for all cross pairs says $A[T,V\setminus T]=c|_T\mathbf 1^\top+\mathbf 1\,c|_{V\setminus T}^\top$, a matrix of rank $\le 1$. Conversely any rank-$\le 1$ binary matrix factors as $a(u)\oplus b(v)$, yielding $c$ (unique up to global flip, giving $S$ and $V\setminus S$). $\square$

**Upper bound: $N\le 10$.** Take the single-vertex cut $T=\{v\}$. Its cross block is $1\times(n-1)$, rank $\le 1$ always, so some disconnecting switch has size
$$|S|=\min\{\deg(v),\,n-\deg(v)\}.$$
(With $c(v)=0$: $S=N(v)$, $|S|=\deg v$; with $c(v)=1$: $S=\{v\}\cup\overline{N(v)}$, $|S|=n-\deg v$.) Thus
$$f(G)\le \min_v\min\{\deg(v),n-\deg(v)\}.$$
Suppose this minimum were $\ge 11$ for some connected $G$ with $100$ edges. Then $11\le\deg(v)\le n-11$ for every $v$, forcing $n\ge 22$ and $\sum_v\deg(v)\ge 11n\ge 242>200=2|E|$, contradiction. Hence for every $G$ with $100$ edges, $f(G)\le 10$ (disconnected graphs have $f=0$). So $N\le 10$.

**Lower bound: $N\ge 10$ via $K_{10,10}$.** Let $G=K_{10,10}$ with parts $A,B$, $|A|=|B|=10$; it has $100$ edges and is connected. We show every disconnecting switch uses exactly $10$ vertices.

By the Lemma, a disconnecting switch corresponds to a nontrivial $T$ with $A[T,V\setminus T]$ of rank $\le 1$. Write $a=|T\cap A|$, $b=|T\cap B|$. Rows of the cross block come in two types:
- row in $A\cap T$: $(\underbrace{0,\dots,0}_{10-a},\underbrace{1,\dots,1}_{10-b})=:r_A$,
- row in $B\cap T$: $(\underbrace{1,\dots,1}_{10-a},\underbrace{0,\dots,0}_{10-b})=:r_B$.

All $A$-rows equal $r_A$, all $B$-rows equal $r_B$, so rank $\le 2$. Rank $\le 1$ requires $r_A,r_B$ dependent: $r_A=0$, or $r_B=0$, or $r_A=r_B$. Now $r_A=0\Leftrightarrow b=10$; $r_B=0\Leftrightarrow a=10$; $r_A=r_B\Leftrightarrow a=b=10$ (trivial $T=V$). So for **nontrivial** $T$, rank $\le 1$ iff $a=10$ or $b=10$, i.e. $T$ contains all of $A$ or all of $B$.

*Case $a=10$:* $T=A\cup B'$ with $B'\subseteq B$, $0\le|B'|=b\le 9$. Columns are $B\setminus B'\subseteq B$. For $v\in A,w\in B\setminus B'$: $A[v,w]=1=c(v)\oplus c(w)$, so all of $B\setminus B'$ gets $c=1\oplus c(A)$. For $v\in B',w\in B\setminus B'$: $A[v,w]=0=c(v)\oplus c(w)$, so $B'$ gets the same value as $B\setminus B'$. Hence all of $B$ gets one color and all of $A$ the other: $S=A$ or $S=B$, so $|S|=10$.

*Case $b=10$:* symmetric, $|S|=10$.

Thus **every** disconnecting switch of $K_{10,10}$ has size $10$ (and $S=B$ indeed works: $K_{10,10}\oplus\delta(B)$ removes the whole $A|B$ cut, leaving $20$ isolated vertices). Therefore $f(K_{10,10})=10$, giving $N\ge 10$.

**Conclusion.** Combining $N\le 10$ and $N\ge 10$,

$$\boxed{N=10}.$$

